<Note
  track="ml"
  slug="linear-regression"
  lede="Linear regression predicts a number as a weighted sum of features. It is the simplest model that learns, the one interviewers ask about most, and the pattern that every bigger model repeats inside itself. This note builds it from a line through three points to the matrix formula, solves it two ways, and shows how to read and distrust the result."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why this note matters</h2>
  <p>
    “Explain linear regression” is the most common opening question in ML interviews, because a good answer touches
    everything: the model, the loss, how it is optimized, what the coefficients mean, what can go wrong. It is also the
    model you should fit first on any numeric problem, because it trains in milliseconds and gives you a baseline that
    a fancier model must beat. And every layer of a <Term id="neural-network">neural network</Term> (week 5) is
    literally this model with many outputs, so the shapes and the update rule you learn here are reused in week 5
    without change.
  </p>
  <p>Questions that are really this note:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Explain linear regression, how it is trained, and its assumptions.”</li>
    <li>“Why do we minimize squared error rather than absolute error?”</li>
    <li>“When would you use the closed-form solution and when gradient descent?”</li>
    <li>“What does R² mean? Can it be negative?”</li>
    <li>“The coefficient for a feature is huge. How do you interpret it, and when should you not?”</li>
    <li>“Is a polynomial fit still linear regression?”</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout kind="note" label="The scope rule">
    <p>
      The model, the squared-error loss, the two ways to solve it, interpretation, assumptions and feature
      transformations. Statistical inference (p-values, confidence intervals on coefficients) is left out; AI
      engineering interviews rarely ask for it. Regularized variants get their own note in week 3.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Put the three house sales from the last note on a chart: size across, price up. Now lay a ruler across the dots
      and slide it around until it runs as close to all of them as it can. That ruler is a linear regression. Its
      slope says “each extra thousand square feet adds about 180 thousand to the price”, and where it crosses the
      left edge says what a house of zero size would cost, which is a bookkeeping number, not a real house.
    </p>
    <p>
      “As close as it can” has to be made exact, and the choice everyone makes is: measure each dot’s vertical gap to
      the ruler, square it, add them up, and pick the ruler that makes the total smallest. Squaring punishes big gaps
      much more than small ones, so the ruler is pulled towards the outliers a little more than you might expect.
      That recipe is called <em>least squares</em>, and it is two hundred years old.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Everyday idea</th><th>Technical term</th><th>Symbol</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The ruler</td><td><span class="term">linear model</span> (regression line, hyperplane)</td><td><Math tex="\hat{y} = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>How steep it is per feature</td><td><span class="term">coefficient</span> (weight, slope)</td><td><Math tex="w_j" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where it crosses the left edge</td><td><span class="term">intercept</span> (bias)</td><td><Math tex="b" /> or <Math tex="w_0" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>A dot’s vertical gap to the ruler</td><td><span class="term">residual</span></td><td><Math tex="r_i = y_i - \hat{y}_i" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Average of the squared gaps</td><td><span class="term">mean squared error</span> (MSE)</td><td><Math tex="\frac{1}{n}\sum_i (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pick the ruler with the smallest total</td><td><span class="term">ordinary least squares</span> (OLS)</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The formula that gives it directly</td><td><span class="term">normal equation</span> (closed form)</td><td><Math tex="\theta = (X^\top X)^{-1} X^\top \mathbf{y}" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>How much of the spread the ruler explains</td><td><span class="term">R²</span> (coefficient of determination)</td><td><Math tex="R^2 = 1 - \text{SS}_\text{res}/\text{SS}_\text{tot}" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="model">The model: a line, then a hyperplane</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      With one feature the model is a line: price = slope × size + intercept. With two features it becomes a flat
      sheet floating over a table of (size, age) pairs. With ten features you cannot draw it, but the formula is the
      same: multiply each feature by its own weight, add them all up, add the intercept. That is a dot product plus a
      number, which you met in the <a href="/ai/notes/math/vectors-dot-product/">vectors note</a>. Linear regression is
      the dot product used as a predictor.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>With one feature:</p>
  <Math block tex="\hat{y} = w\,x + b" />
  <p>With <Math tex="d" /> features <Math tex="\mathbf{x} = (x_1, \dots, x_d)" />:</p>
  <Math block tex="\hat{y} = w_1 x_1 + w_2 x_2 + \dots + w_d x_d + b = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />
  <p>
    The parameters are the weight vector <Math tex="\mathbf{w}" /> and the intercept <Math tex="b" />,
    <Math tex="d + 1" /> numbers in all. The model is called <span class="term">linear</span> because the prediction
    is a weighted sum of the inputs: double a feature’s value and its contribution exactly doubles, and the effect of
    one feature does not depend on the value of another.
  </p>
  <p>
    A small trick makes the formulas cleaner and is used in every textbook: pretend there is an extra feature
    <Math tex="x_0" /> that is always equal to 1, and call the intercept <Math tex="w_0" />. Then
    <Math tex="\hat{y} = w_0 \cdot 1 + w_1 x_1 + \dots = \boldsymbol{\theta}\cdot\mathbf{x}" /> with no separate
    <Math tex="b" />. For a whole dataset, stack the examples as rows of a table (a matrix) <Math tex="X" /> of shape
    <Math tex="n \times (d+1)" /> whose first column is all ones, and every prediction at once is one matrix-vector
    product: every row of <Math tex="X" /> dotted with <Math tex="\boldsymbol{\theta}" />, the “many dot products at
    once” idea from the <a href="/ai/notes/math/vectors-dot-product/#many">end of the vectors note</a>. (The
    <a href="/ai/notes/math/matrices-and-shapes/">matrices note</a>, next in the reading order, does the bookkeeping of
    shapes properly.)
  </p>
  <Math block tex={String.raw`\hat{\mathbf{y}} = X\boldsymbol{\theta} \qquad \text{for the houses: } \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1.5 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} b \\ w \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} b + w \\ b + 1.5w \\ b + 2w \end{pmatrix}`} />
  <Figure n={1} caption="The least-squares line for the three houses, ŷ = 180 x + 23.3. The red ticks are the residuals, y − ŷ: −3.3, +6.7, −3.3. They sum to zero, as least-squares residuals always do when there is an intercept.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 770 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatter of three houses with the best-fit line and the vertical residuals marked">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${fit.x} ${sy(fit, 0)} H${fit.x + fit.w} M${sx(fit, 0.5)} ${fit.y} V${fit.y + fit.h}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={lineD(fit, W, B)} stroke-width="2.5"></path>
        {houses.map((h) => <path class="stroke ink-red" d={`M${sx(fit, h.x)} ${sy(fit, h.y)} V${sy(fit, W * h.x + B)}`} stroke-width="3" />)}
        {houses.map((h) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(fit, h.x)} cy={sy(fit, h.y)} r="7" />)}
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(fit, 0.5)} cy={sy(fit, W * 0.5 + B)} r="4" style="fill: var(--ink)"></circle>
      </g>
      {houses.map((h, i) => <text class="small label-red" x={sx(fit, h.x) + 12} y={sy(fit, (h.y + W * h.x + B) / 2) + 4}>{['−3.3', '+6.7', '−3.3'][i]}</text>)}
      <text class="small" x={sx(fit, 2.5)} y={sy(fit, 0) + 20} text-anchor="end">size x (thousand sq ft)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(fit, 0.5) - 6} y={fit.y - 8} text-anchor="end">price y</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="90">ŷ = 180 x + 23.3</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="122">slope w = 180: +180k per</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="142">extra thousand sq ft</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="174">intercept b = 23.3: the line’s</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="194">height at x = 0 (not a house)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="500" y="226">residuals sum to 0</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="500" y="246">MSE = (3.3² + 6.7² + 3.3²)/3 = 22.2</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Linear regression models the target as a weighted sum of the features plus an intercept,
      <Math tex="\hat{y} = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />. Absorbing the intercept into a constant feature, the
      predictions for a whole dataset are <Math tex="X\boldsymbol{\theta}" />, with <Math tex="X" /> of shape
      <Math tex="n \times (d+1)" />.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="loss">The loss: why squared errors</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      You need one number that says how bad a ruler is. Take every dot’s gap to the ruler. You cannot just add the
      gaps, because gaps above and below would cancel and a terrible ruler could score zero. You could add their
      absolute sizes, which works and has its own uses. But squaring them first has three gifts: it treats above and
      below the same, it makes the score a smooth bowl with one bottom that you can find exactly with a formula, and
      it cares more about one big miss than several small ones, which is usually what you want.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>The <span class="term">mean squared error</span> over <Math tex="n" /> examples is</p>
  <Math block tex="L(\mathbf{w}, b) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\big(y_i - (\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x}_i + b)\big)^2 = \frac{1}{n}\lVert\mathbf{y} - X\boldsymbol{\theta}\rVert^2" />
  <p>
    The second form uses the <a href="/ai/notes/math/norms-distance-cosine/">L2 norm</a>: the MSE is the squared
    Euclidean distance between the vector of true values and the vector of predictions, divided by
    <Math tex="n" />. The quantities <Math tex="r_i = y_i - \hat{y}_i" /> are the <span class="term">residuals</span>,
    and the loss is their mean square. Four reasons squared error is the default, each of which an interviewer may
    ask for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>It is smooth with a single minimum.</strong> As a function of the parameters, MSE is a parabola in each
      one and a bowl overall (Fig. 2). There is exactly one bottom, no local traps, and the slope exists everywhere
      (the technical word is <Term id="convex">convex</Term>). Absolute error has a sharp corner at zero, which makes
      both the formula and gradient descent messier.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It has a closed-form solution.</strong> “Find where the bowl is flat” can be written as equations you
      can solve directly. The next section does it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It is what “most likely” gives you under bell-curve noise.</strong> If the truth is a line plus random
      wobble shaped like a bell curve, then the line that makes the observed prices most probable is exactly the
      least-squares line. The <a href="/ai/notes/math/maximum-likelihood/">maximum likelihood note</a> in week 3 derives
      this; it is the deep reason behind the choice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It penalizes large errors heavily.</strong> An error of 10 costs 100; two errors of 5 cost 50. If big
      misses are much worse than small ones in your application, this is right. If your data has outliers you do not
      trust, it is wrong, and you switch to absolute error or a hybrid; the <a href="/ai/notes/ml/loss-functions/">loss
      functions note</a> next week compares them.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Figure n={2} caption="The loss as a function of the slope w, with the intercept held at its best value 23.3. A parabola with its bottom at w = 180 and height 22.2 there. In both parameters together it is a bowl.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Parabola of mean squared error against slope with the minimum marked at w equals 180">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${bowl.x} ${sy(bowl, 0)} H${bowl.x + bowl.w} M${sx(bowl, 150)} ${bowl.y} V${bowl.y + bowl.h}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={curve(bowl, lossW)} stroke-width="3"></path>
        {bowlPts.map((w) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(bowl, w)} cy={sy(bowl, lossW(w))} r="5" />)}
        <path class="stroke" d={`M${sx(bowl, 165)} ${sy(bowl, 560)} L${sx(bowl, 172)} ${sy(bowl, 320)}`} marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={`M${sx(bowl, 195)} ${sy(bowl, 560)} L${sx(bowl, 188)} ${sy(bowl, 320)}`} marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      {bowlPts.map((w) => <text class="small" x={sx(bowl, w)} y={sy(bowl, 0) + 20} text-anchor="middle">{w}</text>)}
      {bowlPts.map((w) => <text class="small" x={sx(bowl, w) + (w === 180 ? 0 : w < 180 ? -12 : 12)} y={sy(bowl, lossW(w)) - 12} text-anchor={w === 180 ? 'middle' : w < 180 ? 'end' : 'start'}>{lossW(w).toFixed(0)}</text>)}
      <text class="small" x={sx(bowl, 210)} y={sy(bowl, 0) + 20} text-anchor="end">w</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(bowl, 150) - 6} y={bowl.y - 8} text-anchor="end">MSE</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(bowl, 180)} y={sy(bowl, 22) + 40} text-anchor="middle">minimum: w = 180, MSE = 22.2</text>
      <text class="small" x="520" y="100">downhill from either side</text>
      <text class="small" x="520" y="122">leads to the same bottom:</text>
      <text class="small" x="520" y="144">no local minima</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “The loss is mean squared error, the average squared residual. It is convex and smooth, has a closed-form
      minimizer, and corresponds to <Term id="likelihood">maximum likelihood</Term> under Gaussian noise; the price is
      sensitivity to outliers, which is when I would consider absolute or Huber loss.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="closed-form">Solving it exactly: least squares</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Because the loss is a bowl, the best ruler is at the single point where the bowl is flat, and “flat” can be
      written as an equation and solved. For one feature the answer is two small formulas you can do by hand: the
      slope is how much <Math tex="x" /> and <Math tex="y" /> move together, divided by how much <Math tex="x" /> moves
      on its own; the intercept then makes the line pass through the centre of the dots. For many features the same
      idea becomes one matrix formula.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    For one feature, write <Math tex="\bar{x}" /> and <Math tex="\bar{y}" /> for the means. The least-squares slope
    and intercept are:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="w = \frac{\sum_i (x_i - \bar{x})(y_i - \bar{y})}{\sum_i (x_i - \bar{x})^2} \qquad b = \bar{y} - w\,\bar{x}" />
  <p>
    The numerator of <Math tex="w" /> is how <Math tex="x" /> and <Math tex="y" /> vary <em>together</em> (the
    covariance, up to a constant) and the denominator is how <Math tex="x" /> varies on its own (its variance). The
    intercept formula says the line passes through the point of means <Math tex="(\bar{x}, \bar{y})" />. Work the
    houses:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th><Math tex="x_i" /></th><th><Math tex="y_i" /></th><th><Math tex="x_i - \bar{x}" /></th><th><Math tex="y_i - \bar{y}" /></th><th>product</th><th><Math tex="(x_i - \bar{x})^2" /></th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>200</td><td>−0.5</td><td>−93.33</td><td>46.67</td><td>0.25</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.5</td><td>300</td><td>0</td><td>6.67</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>380</td><td>0.5</td><td>86.67</td><td>43.33</td><td>0.25</td></tr>
        <tr><td colspan="2">means: 1.5, 293.33</td><td></td><td></td><td><strong>sum 90</strong></td><td><strong>sum 0.5</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <Math block tex="w = \frac{90}{0.5} = 180 \qquad b = 293.33 - 180 \times 1.5 = 23.33" />
  <p>
    So the best line is <Math tex="\hat{y} = 180x + 23.3" />, with predictions 203.3, 293.3, 383.3 and MSE
    <Math tex="(3.33^2 + 6.67^2 + 3.33^2)/3 = 22.2" />. No search was needed; the formula went straight to the bottom
    of the bowl. The reason the formula is true is that the derivative of the loss is zero at the minimum; the
    <a href="/ai/notes/math/partial-derivatives-gradient/">gradient note</a> next week gives you the tools to derive it
    yourself in ten lines.
  </p>
  <p>
    For many features, the same “find where the bowl is flat” step produces the <span class="term">normal
    equation</span>. Two matrix moves appear in it that the <a href="/ai/notes/math/matrices-and-shapes/">matrices
    note</a> (next in the reading order) makes precise, so here is the one-line version of each: <Math tex="X^\top" />,
    the <em>transpose</em>, is the table flipped so its rows become columns; and a superscript <Math tex="-1" /> on a
    square matrix means its <em>inverse</em>, the matrix that undoes it, the way multiplying by <Math tex="1/5" />
    undoes multiplying by 5 (in code, <code>np.linalg.inv</code>). With <Math tex="X" /> the
    <Math tex="n \times (d+1)" /> data matrix (ones column included) and <Math tex="\mathbf{y}" /> the
    <Math tex="n" /> targets:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\boldsymbol{\theta} = (X^\top X)^{-1} X^\top \mathbf{y}" />
  <Figure n={3} caption="Shapes in the normal equation for n examples and d features (plus the ones column). XᵀX is a small square matrix, (d+1) × (d+1), whatever n is; inverting it costs about (d+1)³ operations.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 760 240" role="img" aria-label="Rectangles showing the shapes of X transpose, X, their product, its inverse, X transpose y and theta">
      <g class="sketch">
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="30" y="90" width="150" height="50" rx="4"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="200" y="40" width="50" height="150" rx="4"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-green" x="290" y="90" width="50" height="50" rx="4"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="400" y="90" width="150" height="50" rx="4"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-blue" x="570" y="40" width="22" height="150" rx="4"></rect>
        <rect class="stroke fill-paper" x="690" y="90" width="22" height="50" rx="4"></rect>
      </g>
      <text x="105" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">Xᵀ</text>
      <text class="small" x="105" y="160" text-anchor="middle">(d+1) × n</text>
      <text x="225" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">X</text>
      <text class="small" x="225" y="210" text-anchor="middle">n × (d+1)</text>
      <text x="270" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">=</text>
      <text x="315" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">XᵀX</text>
      <text class="small" x="315" y="160" text-anchor="middle">(d+1)²</text>
      <text class="small" x="315" y="30" text-anchor="middle">invert this</text>
      <text x="475" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">Xᵀ</text>
      <text class="small" x="475" y="160" text-anchor="middle">(d+1) × n</text>
      <text x="581" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">y</text>
      <text class="small" x="581" y="210" text-anchor="middle">n</text>
      <text x="640" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18">→</text>
      <text x="701" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16">θ</text>
      <text class="small" x="701" y="160" text-anchor="middle">d+1</text>
      <text class="small" x="370" y="120" text-anchor="middle">·</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Read it with the shape rule. <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is <Math tex="(d+1) \times (d+1)" />, a small square matrix however many examples you have; <Math tex="X^\top\mathbf{y}" /> is a <Math tex="(d+1)" />-vector; the inverse times the vector is the <Math tex="(d+1)" /> parameters. For the houses, <Math tex="d = 1" />, so it is a 2 × 2 inverse, and the <a href="#code">code</a> shows it giving the same 23.33 and 180. Three practical notes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cost.</strong> Forming <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is about <Math tex="n d^2" /> operations and inverting it
      about <Math tex="d^3" />. With a million rows and a hundred features that is instant. With a hundred thousand
      features (text with one feature per word) the inverse alone is <Math tex="10^{15}" /> operations, and you use
      gradient descent instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When it breaks.</strong> <Math tex="X^\top X" /> has no inverse if some feature is an exact combination of
      others (a column of temperatures in Celsius next to one in Fahrenheit, or more features than examples). The
      model is then not unique. Adding a penalty on big weights (<Term id="regularization">regularization</Term>,
      week 3) or using <code>lstsq</code>, which returns the shortest of the tied solutions, handles it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>In code, do not invert.</strong> <code>np.linalg.lstsq</code> or <code>solve</code> is more stable and
      faster than <code>inv</code>. The explicit inverse is for the whiteboard.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PaFPbb66DxQ"
    title="StatQuest: The Main Ideas of Fitting a Line to Data (Least Squares)"
    meta="About 9 minutes. The bowl, the “rotate the line until the sum of squares is smallest” picture, and why the derivative being zero finds it."
  />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability/describing-relationships-quantitative-data"
    title="Khan Academy: Describing relationships in quantitative data"
    meta="The “least-squares regression” and “residuals” exercise sets. Do them by hand once; they are exactly the table above with different numbers."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Ordinary least squares has a closed form: the normal equation <Math tex="\theta = (X^\top X)^{-1}X^\top y" />,
      which comes from setting the slope of the MSE to zero. It costs about <Math tex="nd^2 + d^3" />, so it is the
      right choice for moderate <Math tex="d" />; for very many features, or when <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is singular
      because of collinear features, I use gradient descent or add a <Term id="regularization">regularization</Term>
      penalty.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="gradient-descent">Solving it by steps: gradient descent</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      The other way to find the bottom of the bowl is to stand somewhere on it, feel which way is downhill, take a
      small step that way, and repeat. For a bowl this always works and always reaches the same bottom the formula
      finds. It is slower for small problems and the only option for huge ones, and it is the method every big model
      is trained with, so linear regression is the right place to meet it.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    “Which way is downhill” is the <Term id="gradient">gradient</Term>, the subject of week 2. For MSE on a line it
    comes out as two simple expressions, which you can take on trust for one week (the symbol <Math tex="\partial" />
    just means “the slope of the loss when only this one parameter moves”):
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\frac{\partial L}{\partial w} = \frac{2}{n}\sum_i (\hat{y}_i - y_i)\,x_i \qquad \frac{\partial L}{\partial b} = \frac{2}{n}\sum_i (\hat{y}_i - y_i)" />
  <p>
    Read them: each is an average of the errors, weighted by the input for <Math tex="w" /> and unweighted for
    <Math tex="b" />. If predictions are too high on average, <Math tex="\partial L/\partial b" /> is positive and the
    update below pushes <Math tex="b" /> down. The update rule, with a <span class="term">learning rate</span>
    <Math tex="\eta" /> (“eta”) that sets the step size:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="w \leftarrow w - \eta\,\frac{\partial L}{\partial w} \qquad b \leftarrow b - \eta\,\frac{\partial L}{\partial b}" />
  <p>One step by hand, starting from <Math tex="w = 200, b = 0" /> (loss 133.3) with <Math tex="\eta = 0.1" />:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Predictions 200, 300, 400; errors <Math tex="\hat{y} - y" /> are 0, 0, +20.</li>
    <li><Math tex="\partial L/\partial w = \frac{2}{3}(0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 1.5 + 20 \cdot 2) = 26.67" />; <Math tex="\partial L/\partial b = \frac{2}{3}(20) = 13.33" />.</li>
    <li>New <Math tex="w = 200 - 0.1 \times 26.67 = 197.33" />; new <Math tex="b = 0 - 0.1 \times 13.33 = -1.33" />.</li>
    <li>New predictions 196, 294.7, 393.3; errors −4, −5.3, +13.3; new loss <Math tex="(16 + 28.4 + 177.8)/3 = 74.1" />. Down from 133.3 in one step.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Keep going and the pair drifts to <Math tex="(180, 23.3)" />, the closed-form answer, as the
    <a href="#code">code</a> shows. Two things to notice now and study properly next week: the learning rate matters
    (too small and it crawls, too large and it overshoots and diverges), and the two parameters move at very
    different speeds here because <Math tex="x" /> and the constant 1 have different scales. That second problem is
    why the <a href="/ai/notes/ml/feature-scaling-encoding/">feature scaling note</a> exists.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/linear-regression"
    title="Google Machine Learning Crash Course: Linear regression"
    meta="The linear regression unit, including the loss page and the interactive gradient-descent exercise where you adjust the learning rate and watch the line move. Thirty minutes."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Alternatively I minimize the MSE iteratively with gradient descent: the gradient with respect to the weights is
      the average of residuals times inputs, and I step against it with a learning rate. For a convex loss like this
      it converges to the same optimum as the closed form, and it scales to large feature counts and streaming data.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="interpret">Reading the result: coefficients and R²</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A fitted line gives you two kinds of information: what each feature does, read from its coefficient, and how
      well the whole thing explains the data, read from a score between zero and one. Both are easy to misread. A
      coefficient’s size depends on the feature’s units, so a big number can mean a tiny effect measured in small
      units. And the score can be high for a model that is useless on new data, or low for a model that is the best
      anyone could do on noisy data.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <strong>Coefficients.</strong> <Math tex="w_j" /> is the change in the prediction for a one-unit increase in
    feature <Math tex="j" />, <em>holding the other features fixed</em>. For the houses, <Math tex="w = 180" /> means
    “180 thousand more per extra thousand square feet”. Three cautions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Units.</strong> Measure size in square feet instead of thousands and the coefficient becomes 0.18. Same
      model, same predictions, coefficient a thousand times smaller. To compare the importance of features, fit on
      <Term id="feature-scaling">standardized</Term> features (each scaled to mean 0, standard deviation 1); then each
      coefficient is the effect of one standard deviation, in the target’s units, and they are comparable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Correlated features.</strong> When two features move together (house size and number of rooms), the
      model can split the credit between them almost arbitrarily. Individual coefficients become unstable and can
      even flip sign, while predictions stay fine. This is <span class="term">multicollinearity</span>; it is a
      problem for interpretation, not for prediction, and the fix is to drop or combine features, or to add a penalty
      on big weights (week 3).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not causation.</strong> The coefficient says what the data associates with what. Ice-cream sales have a
      positive coefficient for drowning deaths; the hidden feature is summer. Do not say “increasing X by one unit
      <em>causes</em> Y to rise by <Math tex="w" />” unless the data came from an experiment.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>R².</strong> The <span class="term">coefficient of determination</span> compares the model’s residuals with
    those of the dumbest baseline, always predicting the mean <Math tex="\bar{y}" />:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="R^2 = 1 - \frac{\text{SS}_\text{res}}{\text{SS}_\text{tot}} = 1 - \frac{\sum_i (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}{\sum_i (y_i - \bar{y})^2}" />
  <p>
    For the houses, <Math tex="\text{SS}_\text{res} = 66.7" /> and <Math tex="\text{SS}_\text{tot} = 93.33^2 + 6.67^2 + 86.67^2 = 16{,}267" />,
    so <Math tex="R^2 = 1 - 66.7/16{,}267 = 0.996" />: the line explains 99.6% of the variation in price. In words,
    <Math tex="R^2" /> is “the fraction of the target’s variance the model accounts for”. Four facts about it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>On the training data, <Math tex="R^2 = 1" /> is a perfect fit and <Math tex="R^2 = 0" /> is no better than predicting the mean.</li>
    <li>On <em>test</em> data it can be <strong>negative</strong>: the model is doing worse than the test set’s mean. Negative test <Math tex="R^2" /> is a loud sign of overfitting or of a data shift.</li>
    <li>Adding any feature never lowers training <Math tex="R^2" />, even a random one, so it rewards bloat. <span class="term">Adjusted R²</span> penalizes the feature count; better still, judge on held-out data.</li>
    <li>A low <Math tex="R^2" /> is not necessarily a bad model: if the target is genuinely noisy, 0.3 may be the best achievable and still valuable. Compare with a baseline and the domain, not with 1.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For reporting, also quote the <span class="term">RMSE</span>, the square root of the MSE, because it is in the
    target’s own units: an RMSE of 4.7 here means “typically off by about 4.7 thousand”. Interviewers like a candidate
    who reports both a relative score and an absolute one.
  </p>
  <Figure n={4} caption="Residual plots, the fastest check of a fit. Left: residuals scattered evenly around zero, the line has captured the pattern. Right: a U shape, the relationship is curved and the line is missing it.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 740 280" role="img" aria-label="Two residual plots: one with no pattern, one with a U-shaped pattern">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${resL.x} ${sy(resL, 0)} H${resL.x + resL.w}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${resR.x} ${sy(resR, 0)} H${resR.x + resR.w}`}></path>
        {goodRes.map((p) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(resL, p.x)} cy={sy(resL, p.y)} r="5" style="fill: var(--ink)" />)}
        {badRes.map((p) => <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(resR, p.x)} cy={sy(resR, p.y)} r="5" style="fill: var(--ink)" />)}
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={curve(resR, (x) => ((x - 5.5) ** 2) / 6 - 1.5)} stroke-dasharray="6 5" stroke-width="2"></path>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={resL.x} y={sy(resL, 0) - 6}>residual = 0</text>
      <text class="small" x={resR.x} y={sy(resR, 0) - 6}>residual = 0</text>
      <text x={resL.x + resL.w / 2} y="262" text-anchor="middle">good: no pattern</text>
      <text x={resR.x + resR.w / 2} y="262" text-anchor="middle">bad: curvature the line missed</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    The residual plot (residuals against the prediction or against each feature) is worth more than any single
    number. A pattern in it means the model is systematically wrong somewhere: a U shape means missing curvature, a
    fan shape means the noise grows with the prediction, a few far-off points mean outliers. The famous
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anscombe%27s_quartet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anscombe’s quartet</a>
    is four datasets with the same line, the same <Math tex="R^2" /> and completely different pictures; look at it
    once and you will never trust a summary number alone again.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2AQKmw14mHM"
    title="StatQuest: R-squared, Clearly Explained"
    meta="About 11 minutes. The “variation around the mean versus variation around the line” picture that makes the formula obvious."
  />
  <Source
    kind="try"
    href="https://mlu-explain.github.io/linear-regression/"
    title="MLU-Explain: Linear Regression (interactive)"
    meta="Drag points, watch the line, the residuals and R² update live. Includes the normal-equation derivation if you want it early."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A coefficient is the expected change in the target per unit change in that feature with the others held fixed;
      it depends on units, so I standardize before comparing features, and I treat it as association, not causation.
      <Math tex="R^2" /> is one minus the residual sum of squares over the total sum of squares, the fraction of
      variance explained; it can be negative on held-out data, it never decreases when features are added, and I
      always pair it with RMSE in the target’s units and a residual plot.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="assumptions">What linear regression assumes</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A ruler is the right tool when the dots really do lie roughly along a straight line, when each dot’s wobble is
      its own business and about the same size everywhere, and when no two measurements are secretly the same thing.
      When those hold, least squares is not just reasonable; it is the best possible linear estimate. When they fail,
      the line still gets drawn, with no error message, and it quietly misleads you. The assumptions are the checklist
      for noticing.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Assumption</th><th>Plainly</th><th>How to check</th><th>What to do if it fails</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Linearity</span></td><td>The target really is a weighted sum of the features (plus noise).</td><td>Residual plot shows curvature.</td><td>Add transformed features (<Math tex="x^2" />, <Math tex="\log x" />, interactions), or use a non-linear model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Independent errors</span></td><td>One example’s wobble tells you nothing about another’s.</td><td>Residuals correlated over time or within groups.</td><td>Time-series or grouped models; at least, split data by group not by row.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Constant variance</span> (homoscedasticity)</td><td>The wobble is about the same size for small and large predictions.</td><td>Fan-shaped residual plot.</td><td>Predict <Math tex="\log y" /> instead of <Math tex="y" />; weighted least squares.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">Normal errors</span></td><td>The wobble is bell-shaped. Needed for confidence intervals, not for the fit itself.</td><td>Histogram or Q-Q plot of residuals.</td><td>Usually ignore for prediction; matters for inference.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="term">No perfect multicollinearity</span></td><td>No feature is an exact combination of others.</td><td><Math tex="X^\top X" /> singular; huge, unstable coefficients; variance inflation factors.</td><td>Drop or combine features; add a penalty on big weights (ridge regression, week 3).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No influential outliers</td><td>No single point is dragging the line.</td><td>Points with huge residuals or extreme <Math tex="x" />.</td><td>Investigate them; consider a robust loss.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two interview-grade remarks. First, the assumptions are needed for <em>interpretation and inference</em> far
    more than for prediction: a linear model with mildly non-normal, mildly heteroscedastic errors still predicts
    fine. Second, the assumption people forget is independence, and it is the one most often violated in practice:
    multiple rows from the same customer, repeated measurements, or time series. It does not bias the line much, but
    it makes you far more confident than you should be, and it is why train/test splits must respect groups.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.statlearning.com/"
    title="James, Witten, Hastie and Tibshirani: An Introduction to Statistical Learning, chapter 3"
    meta="Section 3.3.3, “Potential problems”, is the best short treatment of these assumptions, with plots. Free PDF; about 15 pages."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “OLS assumes a linear relationship, independent errors with constant variance, and no perfect multicollinearity;
      normality of errors is needed only for inference. I check them with residual plots: curvature means add
      non-linear features, a fan means heteroscedasticity, and unstable coefficients point to collinearity, which I
      handle by dropping features or using ridge regression.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="beyond-lines">Curves from a linear model</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      “Linear” sounds like it can only draw straight lines, but the word refers to the <em>parameters</em>, not the
      picture. If you hand the model <Math tex="x" /> and also <Math tex="x^2" /> as two separate features, it will
      draw a parabola, and it is still linear regression: a weighted sum of the features you gave it. You can feed it
      logarithms, products of features, anything you like. The model stays simple and solvable; the cleverness moves
      into choosing the features.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={5} caption="A parabola fitted by ordinary linear regression. The features are x and x²; the model is ŷ = w₁x + w₂x² + b, which is linear in w₁, w₂ and b.">
