  </Figure>
  <p>Common feature transformations, each still fitted by ordinary least squares:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Polynomial features.</strong> Add <Math tex="x^2, x^3, \dots" />. Powerful and dangerous: high degrees overfit violently (the wiggly curve from the last note). Degree 2 or 3 is usually the limit.</li>
    <li><strong>Log transforms.</strong> Use <Math tex="\log x" /> for features that span orders of magnitude (income, population), or predict <Math tex="\log y" /> when the target is positive and skewed (prices, counts). A coefficient on a log feature reads as “effect per percent change”, which is often the natural scale.</li>
    <li><strong>Interactions.</strong> Add <Math tex="x_1 x_2" /> to let the effect of one feature depend on another: the value of an extra bedroom depends on the size of the house.</li>
    <li><strong>One-hot encoding.</strong> Turn a category (city) into several 0/1 features so the model can give each city its own offset. The <a href="/ai/notes/ml/feature-scaling-encoding/">next note</a> covers how.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is also the bridge to <Term id="neural-network">neural networks</Term> (week 5). A network is “linear
    regression on features it invented itself”: its inner <Term id="layer">layers</Term> learn the transformations,
    and the last layer is exactly the weighted sum on this page. Classical feature engineering and deep learning are
    two answers to the same question of which features to hand the final line.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Linear means linear in the parameters, not in the inputs. Polynomial terms, logs, interactions and one-hot
      categories are all fitted by the same least-squares machinery; the model’s capacity comes from the features.
      A neural network can be viewed as linear regression on learned features.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

