  <h2 id="later">Where you will meet this again</h2>
  <Callout kind="note" label="Optional preview">
    <p>A look ahead; unfamiliar words link to the glossary. Skip on a first read.</p>
  </Callout>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 2.</strong> The loss functions note compares squared error with absolute error and Huber; the
      gradient descent note derives the two slope formulas above and explains the learning rate properly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 3.</strong> Logistic regression is this model with the output squashed into a probability. Ridge
      and Lasso are this model with a penalty on big weights (<Term id="regularization">regularization</Term>), and
      maximum likelihood explains where squared error comes from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 5, neural networks.</strong> Every <Term id="layer">layer</Term> is multi-output linear regression:
      a 768-to-3072 layer is 3,072 linear regressions sharing the same 768 inputs, computed as one matrix multiply.
      The “error times input” slope formula above is exactly what <Term id="backpropagation">backpropagation</Term>
      computes for such a layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 7 to 9, language models.</strong> The final step of a language model is a linear regression from
      its internal state onto 50,257 scores (<Term id="logit">logits</Term>), one per possible next token, which the
      scores-to-percentages recipe (<Term id="softmax">softmax</Term>) turns into probabilities.
      <Term id="fine-tuning">Fine-tuning</Term> for classification replaces that last linear step with a new, small
      one. And “linear probes”, the standard tool for asking what a model knows internally, are linear regressions
      fitted from its internal numbers to a label.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The question is simply “explain linear regression”. Here is a complete answer in about ninety seconds that leaves
    the interviewer nothing obvious to add.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “Linear regression predicts a continuous target as a weighted sum of the features plus an intercept,
      <Math tex="\hat{y} = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />. We fit it by minimizing the mean squared error, the
      average squared residual. Squared error is convex and smooth, has a closed-form minimizer, and corresponds to
      maximum likelihood under Gaussian noise; its downside is sensitivity to outliers.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two ways to solve it. The normal equation, <Math tex="\theta = (X^\top X)^{-1}X^\top y" />, gives the
      exact answer in about <Math tex="nd^2 + d^3" /> time and is what I use for moderate numbers of features;
      gradient descent iterates <Math tex="\theta \leftarrow \theta - \eta \nabla L" /> and is what scales to very many
      features or streaming data, and it reaches the same optimum because the loss is convex.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each coefficient is the expected change in the target per unit of that feature with the others held fixed; it
      depends on the feature’s units, so I standardize before comparing them, and I read it as association, not
      causation. I evaluate with RMSE in the target’s units and <Math tex="R^2" />, the fraction of variance explained,
      on held-out data, where <Math tex="R^2" /> can be negative. The model assumes linearity, independent errors with
      constant variance and no perfect collinearity, which I check with residual plots. And linear means linear in
      the parameters: polynomial, log and interaction features give curved fits with the same machinery, which is why
      it is both the first baseline on any numeric problem and the last layer of every neural network.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Why not absolute error?” Not differentiable at zero and no closed form; but it is robust to outliers, and Huber loss gets both.</li>
    <li>“What if <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is not invertible?” Collinear features or <Math tex="d > n" />. Use the pseudo-inverse (lstsq) or ridge regression, which adds <Math tex="\lambda I" /> and always inverts.</li>
    <li>“Why can test R² be negative?” The model’s residuals are larger than the test set’s own variance around its mean: worse than guessing the mean.</li>
    <li>“Two features are highly correlated. What happens?” Predictions are fine, individual coefficients become unstable and uninterpretable. Drop one, combine them, or regularize.</li>
    <li>“Derive the gradient.” <Math tex="\partial L/\partial \mathbf{w} = \frac{2}{n}X^\top(X\theta - y)" />; week 2 shows the steps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="Write the linear regression model for d features in vector form and name the parameters.">
    <p><Math tex="\hat{y} = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />, with weight vector <Math tex="\mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{R}^d" /> and intercept <Math tex="b" />; or <Math tex="\hat{y} = \boldsymbol{\theta}\cdot\mathbf{x}" /> with a constant feature 1 absorbed. <Math tex="d + 1" /> parameters.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is a residual, and what property do least-squares residuals have when the model has an intercept?">
    <p><Math tex="r_i = y_i - \hat{y}_i" />, the vertical gap. With an intercept they sum to zero, because the intercept’s gradient being zero is exactly <Math tex="\sum_i r_i = 0" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Give three reasons squared error is the default loss for regression.">
    <p>Convex and smooth (one minimum, easy optimization); closed-form solution; it is the most-likely line under bell-curve noise (maximum likelihood, week 3). A fourth: it penalizes large errors strongly, which is often desired.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="State the normal equation and the shape of each piece for n examples and d features.">
    <p><Math tex="\theta = (X^\top X)^{-1}X^\top y" />. <Math tex="X" /> is <Math tex="n \times (d+1)" />, <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is <Math tex="(d+1) \times (d+1)" />, <Math tex="X^\top y" /> is a <Math tex="(d+1)" />-vector, <Math tex="\theta" /> is a <Math tex="(d+1)" />-vector.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="When would you choose gradient descent over the closed form, and vice versa?">
    <p>Closed form for moderate <Math tex="d" /> (thousands or fewer): exact and fast. Gradient descent for very large <Math tex="d" />, for data that does not fit in memory or arrives as a stream, or because it is the same machinery every bigger model is trained with.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Write the gradient of the MSE with respect to w for a single feature, and say what it means in words.">
    <p><Math tex="\frac{2}{n}\sum_i(\hat{y}_i - y_i)x_i" />: the average error weighted by the input. Over-predicting on large inputs makes it positive, which pushes <Math tex="w" /> down.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How do you interpret a coefficient, and what two things make that interpretation unreliable?">
    <p>Expected change in the target per unit increase in the feature, other features fixed. Unreliable when features are correlated (multicollinearity splits credit arbitrarily) and when units differ (compare only standardized coefficients). It is also association, not causation.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Define R² and explain how it can be negative.">
    <p><Math tex="R^2 = 1 - \text{SS}_\text{res}/\text{SS}_\text{tot}" />, the fraction of variance explained relative to predicting the mean. On held-out data the model’s squared residuals can exceed the variance around the test mean, making the ratio larger than 1 and <Math tex="R^2" /> negative: worse than the mean baseline.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why does adding a random feature never decrease training R²? What is the fix?">
    <p>The new coefficient can always be set to zero to recover the old fit, so the optimum is at least as good; in practice it fits a little noise and improves slightly. Use adjusted <Math tex="R^2" /> or, better, evaluate on validation data.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Is fitting y = w₁x + w₂x² + b linear regression?">
    <p>Yes. It is linear in the parameters <Math tex="w_1, w_2, b" />; <Math tex="x^2" /> is just a second feature. The normal equation and gradient descent apply unchanged.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="List the main OLS assumptions and name one diagnostic for each.">
    <p>Linearity (residuals vs fitted shows no curve); independent errors (no autocorrelation or grouping in residuals); constant variance (no fan shape); no perfect multicollinearity (invertible <Math tex="X^\top X" />, modest variance inflation); normality only for inference (Q-Q plot).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What does a U-shaped residual plot tell you, and what do you do?">
    <p>The true relationship is curved and the line is systematically under-predicting at the ends and over-predicting in the middle (or the reverse). Add a squared term or another non-linear transformation, or switch model class.</p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>Paper first, then check. Solutions are hidden under each problem.</p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. Fit a least-squares line to x = (1, 2, 3), y = (2, 4, 6). Then to x = (0, 1, 2, 3), y = (1, 3, 2, 4). Give w, b and the MSE for each.">
    <p>
      First: the points are exactly on <Math tex="y = 2x" />, so <Math tex="w = 2, b = 0" />, MSE 0. Second:
      <Math tex="\bar{x} = 1.5, \bar{y} = 2.5" />; numerator <Math tex="(-1.5)(-1.5) + (-0.5)(0.5) + (0.5)(-0.5) + (1.5)(1.5) = 4" />;
      denominator <Math tex="2.25 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 2.25 = 5" />; <Math tex="w = 0.8" />, <Math tex="b = 2.5 - 1.2 = 1.3" />.
      Predictions 1.3, 2.1, 2.9, 3.7; residuals −0.3, 0.9, −0.9, 0.3; MSE <Math tex="1.8/4 = 0.45" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. For the second dataset in problem 1, compute SS_res, SS_tot and R².">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\text{SS}_\text{res} = 0.09 + 0.81 + 0.81 + 0.09 = 1.8" />. <Math tex="\text{SS}_\text{tot} = 2.25 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 2.25 = 5" />.
      <Math tex="R^2 = 1 - 1.8/5 = 0.64" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. Check that the residuals in problem 1 sum to zero and that the line passes through (x̄, ȳ).">
    <p>
      <Math tex="-0.3 + 0.9 - 0.9 + 0.3 = 0" />. At <Math tex="\bar{x} = 1.5" />: <Math tex="0.8 \times 1.5 + 1.3 = 2.5 = \bar{y}" />. ✓
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. Write the X matrix (with the ones column) for the four-point dataset and compute XᵀX and Xᵀy. Solve the 2 × 2 system and confirm w and b.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="X" /> rows: (1, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2), (1, 3). <Math tex="X^\top X = [[4, 6], [6, 14]]" />, <Math tex="X^\top y = [10, 19]" />.
      Solve <Math tex="4b + 6w = 10" /> and <Math tex="6b + 14w = 19" />: from the first <Math tex="b = 2.5 - 1.5w" />; substitute:
      <Math tex="15 - 9w + 14w = 19" />, <Math tex="w = 0.8" />, <Math tex="b = 1.3" />. Same as the formulas.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. Starting from w = 0, b = 0 on the four-point dataset, do one gradient-descent step with learning rate 0.1. Report the new loss.">
    <p>
      Predictions all 0; errors <Math tex="\hat{y} - y = (-1, -3, -2, -4)" />. <Math tex="\partial L/\partial w = \frac{2}{4}(0 - 3 - 4 - 12) = -9.5" />;
      <Math tex="\partial L/\partial b = \frac{2}{4}(-10) = -5" />. New <Math tex="w = 0.95" />, <Math tex="b = 0.5" />. Predictions 0.5, 1.45, 2.4, 3.35;
      errors −0.5, −1.55, 0.4, −0.65; MSE <Math tex="(0.25 + 2.40 + 0.16 + 0.42)/4 = 0.81" />, down from 7.5.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. A model predicts salary from years of experience with coefficient 4,000 per year. Experience is then re-coded in months. What is the new coefficient? Do the predictions change?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="4000/12 = 333.3" /> per month. Predictions are identical; only the units of the coefficient changed. This is why raw coefficients cannot be compared across features.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. n = 10,000 examples, d = 50 features. Estimate the cost of the normal equation. Repeat for d = 100,000 (bag of words). Which method would you use in each case?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="nd^2 = 2.5 \times 10^7" /> plus <Math tex="d^3 = 1.25 \times 10^5" />: trivial, use the closed form. For <Math tex="d = 10^5" />:
      <Math tex="d^3 = 10^{15}" /> and <Math tex="X^\top X" /> alone has <Math tex="10^{10}" /> entries (80 GB). Use gradient descent (or a sparse solver).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. A dataset has temperature in Celsius and in Fahrenheit as two features. What happens to XᵀX and to the coefficients? Name two fixes.">
    <p>
      The columns are exactly linearly related (<Math tex="F = 1.8C + 32" /> with the ones column), so <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is singular and the coefficients are not unique: any split of the effect between the two works. Drop one feature, or use ridge regression, or lstsq’s minimum-norm solution.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. On a test set, SS_res = 120 and SS_tot = 100. What is R², and what does it mean?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="R^2 = 1 - 1.2 = -0.2" />. The model is worse than predicting the test mean: it has overfit or the test data differs from training.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. A house-price model has RMSE 45 (thousand) and R² 0.85 on validation. A colleague says R² should be above 0.95 before shipping. How do you respond?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="R^2" /> has no universal threshold; it depends on how noisy prices are. Compare with the baseline and with a stronger model; report RMSE in dollars against what the business needs; check the residual plot for systematic errors. 0.85 may be excellent for this problem.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="11. Which of these are linear regression (linear in parameters)? (a) ŷ = w₁x + w₂x² + b; (b) ŷ = w log x + b; (c) ŷ = e^(wx); (d) ŷ = w₁x₁ + w₂x₁x₂ + b; (e) ŷ = x^w.">
    <p>
      (a), (b), (d) yes: weighted sums of fixed transformations of the inputs. (c) and (e) no: the parameter sits inside a non-linear function. (Taking logs of (c) gives <Math tex="\log \hat{y} = wx" />, which is linear regression on a transformed target.)
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="12. Sketch, from memory, the three bad residual plots (curved, fan, outlier) and write one fix next to each.">
    <p>
      Curved (U or arch): add polynomial or log features. Fan (spread grows with prediction): model <Math tex="\log y" />, or weighted least squares. Outliers (a few far points): investigate, remove if erroneous, or use a robust loss such as Huber.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <QA q="13. Implement simple linear regression (two formulas), the normal equation, and gradient descent as three functions. Check all three agree on random data to 4 decimals.">
    <NewInCode items={[
      ["np.linalg.solve(A, b)", "Solves the equations A·θ = b for θ directly. Faster and more accurate than computing an inverse and multiplying.", "numpy/linalg-solve"],
      ["rng.uniform(0, 5, 100)", "100 random numbers spread evenly between 0 and 5. rng is a random generator made once with a seed so the numbers repeat each run.", "numpy/rng-uniform"],
      ["rng.normal(0, 0.5, 100)", "100 random numbers from a bell curve centred on 0 with spread 0.5: the noise added to the true line.", "numpy/rng-normal"],
      ['[("simple", simple_fit), ...]', "Functions are values in Python: the list holds pairs of a name and a function, and the loop calls each one.", ""],
    ]} />
    
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

def simple_fit(x, y):
    w = np.sum((x - x.mean()) * (y - y.mean())) / np.sum((x - x.mean()) ** 2)
    b = y.mean() - w * x.mean()
    return w, b

def normal_eq_fit(x, y):
    X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(x), x])
    # solve the 2 x 2 system; never inv
    b, w = np.linalg.solve(X.T @ X, X.T @ y)
    return w, b

def gd_fit(x, y, lr=0.05, steps=20000):
    w = 0.0
    b = 0.0
    for step in range(steps):
        errors = w * x + b - y
        w = w - lr * 2 * np.mean(errors * x)
        b = b - lr * 2 * np.mean(errors)
    return w, b

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
# 100 random x values between 0 and 5
x = rng.uniform(0, 5, 100)
# true line w = 3, b = -2, plus noise
y = 3 * x - 2 + rng.normal(0, 0.5, 100)

for name, fit in [("simple", simple_fit), ("normal equation", normal_eq_fit), ("gradient descent", gd_fit)]:
    w, b = fit(x, y)
    print(f"{name:>16}: w = {w:.4f}   b = {b:.4f}")

#           simple: w = 2.9869   b = -2.0037
#  normal equation: w = 2.9869   b = -2.0037
# gradient descent: w = 2.9869   b = -2.0037    all three agree, close to the true 3 and -2`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="14. Fit polynomials of degree 1 to 10 to 20 noisy points from a cubic. Plot train and test R² against degree, and mark the degree where test R² is best.">
    <NewInCode items={[
      ["np.polyfit(x, y, degree)", "Fits a polynomial curve (x, x², …, up to x to the degree) by least squares and returns its coefficients. This is linear regression on polynomial features in one call.", "numpy/polyfit"],
      ["np.polyval(coeffs, x)", "Computes that curve at the given x values: the predictions.", "numpy/polyfit"],
      ["x[:20], x[20:]", "Slices: the first 20 elements, and everything from position 20 to the end.", "python/slicing-steps"],
      ["np.argmax(test_r2)", "The position of the largest value, here the degree with the best test R².", "numpy/argmax"],
      ["plt.axvline, plt.legend, plt.xlabel", "Draw a vertical marker line, name the lines, and label the x-axis.", "python/plt-lines"],
    ]} />
    
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.metrics import r2_score

rng = np.random.default_rng(1)
x = rng.uniform(-2, 2, 40)
# the truth is a cubic, plus noise
y = x ** 3 - x + rng.normal(0, 0.5, 40)
# first 20 points for training
x_train = x[:20]
y_train = y[:20]
x_test = x[20:]                                  # last 20 for testing
y_test = y[20:]

degrees = list(range(1, 11))
train_r2 = []
test_r2 = []
for degree in degrees:
    # least squares on x, x², ..., x^degree
    coeffs = np.polyfit(x_train, y_train, degree)
    train_r2.append(r2_score(y_train, np.polyval(coeffs, x_train)))
    test_r2.append(r2_score(y_test, np.polyval(coeffs, x_test)))

best = degrees[np.argmax(test_r2)]
print("best degree on test:", best)
for degree, tr, te in zip(degrees, train_r2, test_r2):
    print(f"degree {degree:>2}: train R² {tr:.3f}   test R² {te:.3f}")
# best degree on test: 6
# degree  1: train R² 0.721   test R² 0.693
# degree  3: train R² 0.978   test R² 0.960    the truth is a cubic: the big jump happens here
# degree  6: train R² 0.980   test R² 0.960    no real gain over degree 3
# degree  8: train R² 0.988   test R² 0.286
# degree 10: train R² 0.992   test R² -1.836   worse than guessing the average
# Train R² only goes up. Test R² jumps at degree 3, stays flat, then
# collapses: overfitting.

plt.plot(degrees, train_r2, label="train R²")
plt.plot(degrees, test_r2, label="test R²")
plt.axvline(best, color="gray", linestyle="--")
plt.xlabel("degree")
plt.legend()
plt.show()`}
