  <QA q="16. Create two perfectly collinear features (Celsius and Fahrenheit) plus noise in y. Show that np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X) fails or gives garbage, that lstsq returns a sensible answer, and that predictions are still fine.">
    <NewInCode items={[
      ["np.linalg.matrix_rank(X)", "How many columns of X carry independent information. Fewer than the number of columns means one column is a copy or mix of others.", "numpy/linalg-rank"],
      ["try: … except np.linalg.LinAlgError", "Run the inverse; if NumPy raises its “impossible matrix” error, print it instead of crashing.", "python/try-except"],
      ["np.ones(200)", "An array of 200 ones: the column that carries the intercept b.", "numpy/ones"],
    ]} />
    
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
c = rng.uniform(-10, 35, 200)                    # temperature in Celsius
# the same temperature in Fahrenheit
f = 1.8 * c + 32
y = 0.5 * c + 3 + rng.normal(0, 0.3, 200)
X = np.column_stack([np.ones(200), c, f])        # ones, Celsius, Fahrenheit

# independent columns: 2, not 3
print(np.linalg.matrix_rank(X))

try:
    theta = np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X) @ X.T @ y
    # runs, but the numbers are garbage
    print("inv gave:", theta.round(1))
    # prints [-2.5 -0.2  0.2]
except np.linalg.LinAlgError as err:
    print("inv failed:", err)

theta, residuals, rank, singular_values = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)
# rank 2
print("lstsq:", theta.round(3), "rank", rank)
# prints [-0.016  0.329  0.094]

rmse = np.sqrt(np.mean((X @ theta - y) ** 2))
print("prediction RMSE:", round(rmse, 3))
# prints 0.306: predictions are fine (the noise was 0.3)
# lstsq splits the effect between c and f: 0.329 + 1.8 * 0.094 = 0.498, the
# true 0.5 per degree C.
# Lesson: collinearity ruins interpretation, not prediction. Drop f, or add
# a penalty (week 3).`}
    />
  </QA>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Comparing raw coefficients to rank feature importance.</strong> They are in different units. Standardize
      first, and even then correlated features share credit unpredictably.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Reading a coefficient as a causal effect.</strong> Observational data gives associations. Say
      “associated with”, not “causes”, unless the data came from an experiment.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Reporting training R².</strong> It only rises as you add features. Report held-out <Math tex="R^2" /> and
      RMSE, and remember held-out <Math tex="R^2" /> can be negative.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Forgetting the intercept.</strong> Without the ones column the line is forced through the origin, the
      residuals no longer sum to zero, and the fit is usually much worse. <code>fit_intercept=True</code> is the default
      for a reason.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Inverting XᵀX in code.</strong> Numerically fragile and slow. Use <code>lstsq</code> or <code>solve</code>;
      keep the explicit inverse for the whiteboard.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Saying linear regression “can only fit straight lines”.</strong> It fits any function that is linear in
      the parameters; curves come from the features. The limitation is that you must choose those features, which is
      what <Term id="neural-network">neural networks</Term> automate.
    </p>
  </Callout>

