  <h2 id="revision">One-minute revision</h2>
  <Callout kind="revision">
    <ul>
      <li>Model: <Math tex="\hat{y} = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b" />; with a ones column, <Math tex="\hat{\mathbf{y}} = X\boldsymbol{\theta}" />, <Math tex="X" /> is <Math tex="n \times (d+1)" />.</li>
      <li>Loss: MSE <Math tex="= \frac{1}{n}\lVert\mathbf{y} - X\theta\rVert^2" />. Convex bowl, one minimum. Squared because: smooth, closed form, Gaussian MLE, punishes big errors.</li>
      <li>Simple case: <Math tex="w = \sum(x-\bar{x})(y-\bar{y}) / \sum(x-\bar{x})^2" />, <Math tex="b = \bar{y} - w\bar{x}" />. Houses: 180 and 23.3, MSE 22.2.</li>
      <li>Normal equation: <Math tex="\theta = (X^\top X)^{-1}X^\top y" />. Cost <Math tex="nd^2 + d^3" />. Fails if collinear; use lstsq or ridge.</li>
      <li>Gradient descent: <Math tex="\partial L/\partial w = \frac{2}{n}\sum(\hat{y}-y)x" />; same optimum; scales to huge <Math tex="d" />.</li>
      <li>Coefficient = change per unit, others fixed; unit-dependent; standardize to compare; association not causation; collinearity makes them unstable.</li>
      <li><Math tex="R^2 = 1 - \text{SS}_\text{res}/\text{SS}_\text{tot}" />; can be negative on test; never falls with more features; pair with RMSE and a residual plot.</li>
      <li>Assumptions: linearity, independent errors, constant variance, no perfect collinearity (normality only for inference). Residual plots catch the first three.</li>
      <li>Linear in parameters, not inputs: polynomial, log, interaction, one-hot features. A network = linear regression on learned features.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
